        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Most pharmaceuticals are metabolized by the liver, and clinically-used medication doses are given with normal liver function in mind. Metabolism, specifically the metabolism of administered medications, is a large part of pharmacokinetics and among the chief determinants of concentration and duration of action of administered medications. Because of this, metabolism can make the difference between a drug that works, and one that doesn’t work or causes side effects. Metabolism is also highly variable; it can be influenced by an individual’s genetic makeup and by a number of environmental factors. Exposure to gamma-irradiation is one of these factors; it has been shown to alter expression of many genes including some that metabolize drugs. Elevated exposure to gamma-irradiation is experienced during space flight.
<p>
The specific aims were as follows:<ol>
<li>To determine if liver gene expression changes from radiation exposure and high dietary iron result in measurable changes in protein expression.
<li>To determine if meaningful changes in metallothionein (previously identified as a very radiation-sensitive molecule) can be detected in serum, raising the possibility of using serum metallothionein as a biomarker for radiation exposure.
<li>To determine if rat liver is a suitable model for the study of radiation-induced gene expression changes in oxidative stress and drug metabolism.</ol>
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
This study investigated gene and protein expression in response to exposure to the space flight environment for 12 days. Expression of a select group of genes was first examined in liver tissue from each group of animals - space flight and ground control. In a second phase, gene products (proteins) were examined for those genes that show significant expression changes. It is not known whether the gene expression changes measured in the previous pilot study are followed by changes in enzyme expression or activities (nor has this been demonstrated adequately in the literature); the protein experiments in this study addressed that question.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
Results showed significant changes in gene expression in many genes associated with metabolism of administered medications, drug transport across cell membranes, reduction-oxidation homeostasis, and repair of DNA damage. Analysis of expressed protein in the samples showed similar trends. 
<p>
        </div>

